<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samaveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sama Veda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samaveda is one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, the sacred texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, consisting of verses drawn mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> and set to melodies for chanting at soma sacrifices. Compiled in the later Vedic period, probably in the Kuru realm of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, it was the special charge of the udgatar priests. It is regarded as a root of Indian classical music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samaveda.jpg" alt="Samaveda" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1636_CE_Samaveda,_Sadvimsha_Brahmana,_Varanasi_Sanskrit_college,_Edward_Cowell_collection,_sample_iii,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Samaveda, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> saman, &quot;song&quot;, and veda, &quot;knowledge&quot;, is the Veda of melodies and chants. It is a liturgical text of 1,875 verses, all but 75 of them taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>. Its earliest parts are believed to date from as early as the Rigvedic period, but the existing samhita belongs to the post-Rigvedic Mantra period. Michael Witzel, who notes that there is no absolute dating for the Vedic texts, estimates it at 1200 to 1000 BCE, roughly contemporary with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>.</p><p>The text has several layers: the Samhita, the oldest, then Brahmana texts and finally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, likely composed around the 6th century BCE. Three recensions survive. The Kauthuma is current in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, the Ranayaniya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> among other regions, and the Jaiminiya in Karnataka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. The Jaiminiya preserves the oldest surviving tradition of Samavedic chanting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Samaveda has two major parts: four collections of melodies and three books of verses. The Gramageya melodies were for public recitation, and the Aranyageya for personal meditative use, as in the solitude of a forest. The verse books divide into the Purvarcika, with 585 single-stanza verses arranged by deity, and the Uttararcika, arranged by ritual. In all there are 1,549 unique verses. The purpose of the text was liturgical, and it was the repertoire of the udgatar, or singer, priests.</p><p>The text is not meant to be read: like a musical score, it must be heard. It contains notated melodies, probably the oldest that survive anywhere. Frits Staal argued that the melodies likely existed before the verses, since some early words fit them well and later ones do not, and that stobha, embellishing syllables, were used to fit words to the music. The Chandogya Upanishad and the Kena Upanishad are embedded in the Samaveda; the Chandogya was cited 810 times by Adi Shankara in his commentary on the Vedanta Sutra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Theodor Benfey published a German edition in 1848, Satyavrata Samashrami a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> one in 1873, Filipp Fortunatov a Russian translation in 1875 and Ralph Griffith an English translation in 1893. The Samaveda has received less attention than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, since all but 75 of its verses derive from it. In the Bhagavad Gita Krishna says, &quot;Among the Vedas I am the Samaveda.&quot; Guy Beck holds that Indian classical music and dance are rooted in its sonic and musical dimensions, and musicologists widely acknowledge its chant theory as a root of Indian music.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samaveda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shruti, “what is heard”, is revealed knowledge received by the ancient seers: the four Vedas, the Rigveda, <b>Samaveda</b>, Yajurveda and Atharvaveda, each with its Brahmanas, ritual commentaries; Aranyakas, forest treatises; and Upanishads, philosophical dialogues.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…Rigveda, by far the most archaic and closest to the Indo-Iranian heritage; the mantra texts of the Atharvaveda, <b>Samaveda</b> and Yajurveda; the Samhita prose of the Black Yajurveda; the Brahmana prose, with the oldest Upanishads; and the Sutras, the last stratum of Vedic Sanskrit before c. 500 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuru kings patronised Brahmin priests, who arranged the hymns of the Rigveda into collections and developed the <b>Samaveda</b> and Yajurveda.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuru-kingdom">Kuru Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
